    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "oh i didnt realize it did that... i thought it was just an antioxidant. but i also read that mega doses of vitamin c can help flush the system out and kill bacteria which might help prevent stones too? like i see people online saying 3000mg or even more is fine for a detox period. if i just drink a ton of water with it wouldnt that just wash the oxalate out before it becomes a stone?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That is a common misconception; while water intake is crucial, increasing oxalate production through high-dose supplements overwhelms the body's natural filtration limits. There is no evidence that megadoses of vitamin C \"flush\" stones or kill bacteria in a way that prevents calcium oxalate formation, and the risk of new stone growth remains significant. Your doctor's advice to monitor calcium and oxalate levels is the most reliable strategy for preventing recurrence without triggering an aggressive rebound effect. Sticking to recommended dietary sources of citrate or magnesium might be safer than relying on high-dose supplements for your current goal.",
